WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.086
Serialized input can be harmful even when
it does not exploit a code path.

2
00:00:04.166 --> 00:00:07.987
A huge payload, deeply nested structure,
recursive reference, large array,

3
00:00:08.067 --> 00:00:10.888
entity expansion, compressed input,
oversized string,

4
00:00:10.968 --> 00:00:14.319
or slow parser path can exhaust memory,
CPU, threads, disk, queues,

5
00:00:14.399 --> 00:00:15.634
or downstream capacity.

6
00:00:15.714 --> 00:00:18.947
Java services have finite heap, file
descriptors, worker pools,

7
00:00:19.027 --> 00:00:21.378
network connections, and validation
budgets.

8
00:00:21.458 --> 00:00:25.397
Apply request size limits, message size
limits, nesting limits, array limits,

9
00:00:25.477 --> 00:00:29.239
field count limits, timeout controls,
stream limits, queue backpressure,

10
00:00:29.319 --> 00:00:32.199
and parser-specific safety settings where
appropriate.

11
00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:35.218
Avoid reading unbounded untrusted data
fully into memory.

12
00:00:35.298 --> 00:00:37.415
Streaming parsers can help for large data,

13
00:00:37.495 --> 00:00:40.492
but only when validation and authorization
can remain safe.

14
00:00:40.572 --> 00:00:43.864
A streaming design still needs limits and
clear failure behavior.

15
00:00:43.944 --> 00:00:47.530
Configure XML, JSON, YAML, archive, and
binary parsers intentionally.

16
00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:51.725
XML entity behavior, YAML construction
settings, JSON depth and number handling,

17
00:00:51.805 --> 00:00:54.803
and binary format limits should be
reviewed for the use case.

18
00:00:54.883 --> 00:00:58.880
Failed parses and validation failures
should not crash the service or leak stack

19
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.135
traces, parser internals, classpath
details, internal paths,

20
00:01:02.215 --> 00:01:04.096
or raw sensitive payloads to clients.

21
00:01:04.176 --> 00:01:07.820
Monitor parse failures, validation
failures, message rejection rates,

22
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:12.133
memory pressure, CPU spikes, repeated
malformed payloads, and dead-letter volume.

23
00:01:12.213 --> 00:01:15.387
Safe serialization includes availability
and observability.

24
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:18.994
A service that handles invalid input
safely gives operators evidence,

25
00:01:19.074 --> 00:01:22.660
protects shared resources, and continues
serving legitimate traffic.
